                    briefing_text_r3:       `Three ideas compete for the same dollars under a <strong>$300k hard cap</strong>: the <strong>pro-desk + 2-truck delivery + trade credit ($140k)</strong> widens the gap with MegaBuild on the segment that matters most; the renovation ($120k) fights head-to-head on selection; the second store ($420k) exceeds your cap, adds ~$180k/yr of overhead, and splits Tessa's attention right as the fight peaks. <em>Concentration beats sprinkling.</em> Which one move, fully funded, makes Granger most defensible?`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Sequence the 9-month plan and defend it`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Sequence the moves so Granger is repositioned <strong>before</strong> MegaBuild opens, then defend the plan to the founder and the SBA lender at <strong>9.5%</strong>. Switching costs are cheapest to build before customers have an alternative — so lock in the contractor relationships first. Show that <em>every borrowed dollar clears 9.5% and improves ROIC</em>, name the break-even, and commit to the metrics that state the bet plainly: margin, contractor retention, market share and ROIC.`,

