                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_contractor:    `<strong>Right place to plant your flag.</strong> Contractors are 45% of sales and the segment MegaBuild serves worst — they value reliability and credit, not the lowest sticker. Defending them is defending your margin, not your foot traffic.`,
                    coach_r1_ranch:         `<strong>Ranch & farm are loyal, but smaller.</strong> They're a reasonable second front, yet the highest-leverage, least-contestable revenue still sits with the contractors. Don't let the safe-feeling segment crowd out the decisive one.`,
                    coach_r1_retail:        `<strong>You're fighting for the customers you'll lose.</strong> Weekend walk-ins are 25% of revenue and exactly what MegaBuild wins on price and selection. Chase them with coupons and you spend money defending the indefensible while contractors get no new reason to stay.`,
                    coach_r1_everyone:      `<strong>"Defend everything" defends nothing.</strong> Spread thin across all three segments and you give MegaBuild the easy ones for free. Strategy follows the margin — pick where you can actually win.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the contractor segment AND the real advantage — service and switching costs MegaBuild can't copy: accounts, credit, the odd part, 26 years of trust. That is exactly where to build before the giant arrives.`,
                    coach_r1_selection:     `<strong>You can't out-stock a 60,000-sq-ft box.</strong> Breadth of inventory is the one thing scale guarantees MegaBuild. Compete on the dimension it undervalues — service and relationships — not the one it dominates.`,
                    coach_r1_price:         `<strong>You cannot be the cheapest in town.</strong> A higher-cost independent that fights a scale player on price simply hands the giant its cost advantage in the form of your losses. Low price is not your durable advantage — service is.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>That's surrender dressed as realism.</strong> Granger has a real, durable edge — 26 years of contractor relationships, credit and reliability MegaBuild serves worst. Hoping the giant stumbles is not a strategy.`,
                    coach_r1_lowclarity:    `<strong>You haven't named what's actually at risk.</strong> Without honestly separating what MegaBuild will simply win from what you can defend, you'll spend capital in the wrong places. Clarity first.`,
                    coach_r2_targeted:      `<strong>Targeted matching protects your price image cheaply.</strong> Matching ~10 visible known-value SKUs stops you looking expensive without surrendering the 38% margin that funds the service you actually compete on.`,
                    coach_r2_hold:          `<strong>Holding the line preserves margin</strong> — defensible if your service story is loud enough. But with zero visible price signals you risk looking expensive on the items shoppers check. Pair it with a few known-value matches.`,
                    coach_r2_blanket:       `<strong>That's about $130k of gross profit gone</strong> — more than half of Tessa's cash flow — to fight a 60,000-sq-ft store on the one thing it's built to win. MegaBuild can out-discount you forever. Where can Granger compete that it can't follow?`,
                    coach_r2_raise:         `<strong>Raising every price hands MegaBuild your price-shoppers.</strong> Add value selectively, yes — but a blanket increase with a giant opening down the road looks tone-deaf and bleeds commodity volume.`,
                    coach_r2_disciplined:   `<strong>This is disciplined local pricing.</strong> Targeted matches protect the image, you add value on service items where you have pricing power, and you refuse the margin-destroying war. That's Schaefer's lesson made operational.`,
                    coach_r2_discount:      `<strong>Discounting your differentiated items throws away your pricing power.</strong> The delivery, expertise and urgent-part service are exactly where you should add value, not cut. You're handing back the margin that funds your moat.`,
                    coach_r2_overimage:     `<strong>You're over-spending on signage.</strong> A few loud known-value signals do the job; pouring cash into price-image is money not spent on the pro-desk that actually builds switching costs.`,
                    coach_r2_noimage:       `<strong>No price-image signal at all is risky.</strong> Shoppers will check a handful of known-value items and decide you're expensive. A small, visible match protects your image without a margin war.`,
                    coach_r2_lowdiscipline: `<strong>Low discipline is how the price war starts.</strong> Every time you flinch and cut to match MegaBuild, you bleed the margin that funds your real advantage. Refuse the fight you cannot win.`,
                    coach_r3_overcap:       `<strong>You've blown through the $300k cap.</strong> Every dollar over draws the SBA line at 9.5%, and you haven't shown me the new break-even. One slow ranch season and the family business is underwater. Stay inside the cap or prove every borrowed dollar clears the hurdle.`,
                    coach_r3_secondstore:   `<strong>The Brigham City store is exciting — and it's a risk move, not a growth move.</strong> It costs $420k against your $300k cap, adds $180k of annual overhead, and splits Tessa's attention exactly when the MegaBuild fight peaks. Can she really run two stores through a price war — and what does it do for you that the pro-desk doesn't?`,
                    coach_r3_sprinkle:      `<strong>A little into each finishes nothing.</strong> A partial renovation, a small pro-desk and a down-payment on a store means no single move is a real advantage when MegaBuild opens. Concentration beats sprinkling — fully fund the one that matters.`,
                    coach_r3_concentrate:   `<strong>That's capital discipline.</strong> Fully funding the pro-desk + delivery concentrates your $300k on the differentiation MegaBuild can't match, on the segment that matters most, inside the cap. One finished advantage beats three half-built ones.`,
                    coach_r3_attention:     `<strong>You're ignoring the binding constraint.</strong> In an owner-run business, Tessa's bandwidth is scarcer than her capital. Funding more than one owner can run well destroys value even when it's affordable.`,
                    coach_r4_prodeskfirst:  `<strong>Right sequence.</strong> Switching costs are cheapest to build before customers have an alternative — locking in the contractor pro-desk and pricing first means Granger arrives at the fight already repositioned, not reacting to it.`,
                    coach_r4_even:          `<strong>Spreading evenly delays the move that matters.</strong> A bit of everything each quarter means the contractor switching costs aren't locked in until MegaBuild is already open. Front-load the decisive move.`,
                    coach_r4_wait:          `<strong>Waiting hands MegaBuild the first move.</strong> You have a 9-month window and switching costs are cheapest to build now, before customers have an alternative. Arrive at the fight unprepared and you lose the cheapest ground you'll ever hold.`,
                    coach_r4_hurdle:        `<strong>That's a lender-ready answer.</strong> Showing every borrowed dollar clears 9.5% and improves ROIC, with a stated break-even, is exactly what defends the plan to the founder and the SBA. You're growing returns, not just size.`,
                    coach_r4_size:          `<strong>"Bigger is safer" is how owners over-extend.</strong> Size adds fixed cost and break-even, not safety. The lender wants to see the return on each borrowed dollar, not the headline footprint.`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>You've added fixed cost without a break-even.</strong> Present revenue upside with no margin math and the SBA lender — and Tessa's father — will rightly ask what slow season sinks the business. Model the break-even or don't borrow.`,
                    coach_r4_revenue:       `<strong>Top-line and store count are vanity metrics here.</strong> They grow the number without telling you whether the bet is working. Track margin, contractor retention, share and ROIC — the metrics that state the bet plainly.`,
                    coach_r4_nokpi:         `<strong>No metrics means no early warning.</strong> "Trust the plan" is how a slipping repositioning goes unnoticed until it's too late. Commit to the KPIs that signal success or trouble.`,

